matiec, the IEC 61131-3 compiler that turns this project's ST into C.

    upstream  https://github.com/beremiz/matiec
    branch    default
    commit    7680ed8e7ffc1a76fa9f9620d6c6e7a3e75c088d
    vendored  2026-09-03

WHY A COPY, AND WHY PINNED
matiec's output is COMMITTED under src/sites/<site>/generated/, and nothing
pinned the compiler that produced it: two developers with different matiec
builds would produce different POUS.c and neither would notice. The pin is what
makes `make -C build check-st` - regenerate, then diff - a meaningful check.

A copy rather than a submodule because this project is developed in git
worktrees, and `git worktree add` does not populate submodules.

WHAT WAS REMOVED, AND WHY
Four directories present upstream are not here. None is a build input, and
dropping them is the whole of the deviation from that commit:

    lib/      matiec's own IEC library, GPLv3, and the one that MATTERS.
              We never load it: `iec2c -I st/lib` points at st/lib/ieclib.txt,
              which is ours, and which exists precisely so that none of
              matiec's function blocks reach the firmware image. Keeping a
              copy here would only invite someone to point -I at it.
    tests/    matiec's own test corpus.
    AnnexF/   the standard's example programs.
    debian/   packaging.

One file was also deleted: matiec's own `.gitignore`, which ignores `config/*`
and `stage1_2/*.cc`. Upstream tracks those anyway - an ignore rule does not
touch a tracked file - but here they are added fresh, so the rule would have
silently dropped twelve autotools scripts and four generated-then-committed
sources, and the vendored tree would not have built on a clone.

`make dist` was tried first and is not usable: the tarball omits header files
the build needs, so it does not compile.

LICENCE
GPLv3 - see COPYING. It is a TOOL: this repository runs the binary and commits
its output. Nothing of matiec's is linked into, or compiled into, the firmware.
The one place that could have happened is its IEC library, which is why st/lib
replaces it and why lib/ is absent above.

BUILDING
`make -C build matiec`, which needs flex, bison, autoconf and automake. Upstream
ships configure.ac and no configure, so autoreconf runs first. Where any of
those is missing the CMake targets are not declared at all and the ordinary
build is unaffected.
